% Electromagnet — a library example (run it with F5; "Make a copy" to change it)
%
% A C-shaped iron core (μr = 1000, 20 mm thick, 100 × 80 mm outside) with a 2 mm air gap in its right leg and a coil
% of N I = 1000 ampere-turns round its left leg, seen end-on (2-D: everything is long in z, results are per metre of
% depth). magnetostatic2d solves for the vector potential A_z in a 180 × 140 mm box of air (A = 0 on the box: no flux
% leaves it) on a 1 mm grid; the canvas shows the flux density B, strongest in the core and across the gap, with some
% flux leaking through the window and bulging out at the gap (fringing).
% Compared with the magnetic-circuit formula B_gap = μ0 N I / (g + l_iron / μr) (one flux path, no leakage, no fringing);
% the inductance and the pull across the gap come from the same field: L' = 2 W' / I² for N turns, F' = B² w / (2 μ0).
% Plotted: B_y along the line y = 0 across the core (left leg, window, gap).
% Units: SI (m, A, T); μ0 = 4π·10⁻⁷ H/m. Change N, I, g, mur or h below and run it again.
% Limits: linear iron (no saturation: real steel saturates near 1.5–2 T), a uniform grid (the gap is g/h elements
% across: finer is closer), static (no eddy currents, no losses). See the help topic "Magnetic fields in 2-D".
clf
mu0 = 4e-7 * pi;
N = 100; I = 10;                  % turns, amperes: N I = 1000 ampere-turns
g = 0.002;                        % air gap (m)
mur = 1000;                       % relative permeability of the core
t = 0.020;                        % core thickness (m)
W = 0.100; H = 0.080;             % core outside (m)
h = 0.001;                        % grid size (m)
box = [0.180 0.140];
m = quadGrid(box(1), box(2), round(box(1) / h), round(box(2) / h), -box / 2);
X = meshNodes(m); Q = meshElems(m);
xc = (X(Q(:, 1), 1) + X(Q(:, 2), 1)) / 2; yc = (X(Q(:, 1), 2) + X(Q(:, 4), 2)) / 2;
% the core: the frame minus its window, minus the gap in the right leg
frame = abs(xc) < W / 2 & abs(yc) < H / 2;
window = abs(xc) < W / 2 - t & abs(yc) < H / 2 - t;
gap = xc > W / 2 - t & abs(yc) < g / 2;
iron = frame & ~window & ~gap;
% the coil round the left leg: its two sides, current out of the page inside the window, into it outside
cw = 0.006; ch = 0.040;            % coil side width and height (m)
inner = xc > -W / 2 + t + 0.001 & xc < -W / 2 + t + 0.001 + cw & abs(yc) < ch / 2;
outer = xc < -W / 2 - 0.001 & xc > -W / 2 - 0.001 - cw & abs(yc) < ch / 2;
J0 = N * I / (sum(inner) * h * h);   % current density (A/m²) for N I through one coil side
J = J0 * inner - J0 * outer;
r = magnetostatic2d(m, 1 + (mur - 1) * iron, J, struct(), struct('show', false));
% shown on the same grid in millimetres, the part's unit, so it is drawn at its real size in the view
mm = quadGrid(1000 * box(1), 1000 * box(2), round(box(1) / h), round(box(2) / h), -500 * box);
show(field(mm, [r.B, zeros(size(r.B, 1), 1)], 'elem', 'B', 'T'), 'name', 'Electromagnet: flux density B');
% the gap: elements inside it, away from its ends
ing = gap & xc > W / 2 - t + h & xc < W / 2 - h;
Bgap = mean(abs(r.B(ing, 2)));
li = 2 * ((W - t) + (H - t)) - g;     % mean iron path
Bc = mu0 * N * I / (g + li / mur);
fprintf('B in the gap %.4f T; magnetic circuit %.4f T (%+.1f %%: fringing and leakage)\n', Bgap, Bc, 100 * (Bgap / Bc - 1));
L = 2 * r.energy / I^2;
Lc = N^2 * mu0 * t / (g + li / mur);
fprintf('inductance %.4g H per metre of depth; the circuit gives %.4g H/m, missing the flux that leaks past the gap\n', L, Lc);
fprintf('stored energy %.4g J/m; |B| peaks at %.2f T in the window''s inner corners (real steel would saturate there)\n', r.energy, max(r.Bmag));
F = Bgap^2 * t / (2 * mu0);
fprintf('pull across the gap about %.0f N per metre of depth (%.1f N for 50 mm deep)\n', F, F * 0.05);
% B_y along y = 0
row = find(abs(yc - h / 2) < h / 4);
[xs, o] = sort(xc(row));
plot(1000 * xs', r.B(row(o), 2)', 'title', 'Electromagnet: B_y along y = 0', 'xlabel', 'x (mm)', 'ylabel', 'B_y (T)');
